 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Sensor and conversion",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "The humidity element sits on the top face of the package, under an open window. Both sensors feed one ADC through a multiplexer; calibration coefficients are held in OTP. The result registers always hold a 14-bit value; humidity can be set to 8, 11 or 14 bits and temperature to 11 or 14 bits; the setting fixes conversion time and accuracy."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Modes",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "Two modes: sleep and measurement. The part powers up asleep and stays there, accepting configuration, status reads, triggers and result reads over I2C. A trigger moves it into measurement; when the conversion ends it drops back to sleep on its own. Sleep draws about 100 nA, and the low current while measuring keeps self-heating small."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Supply monitor",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "BTST (register 0x02, bit 11) reads 1 when the supply is below 2.8 V. It is refreshed after power-on reset and after every measurement request."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Heater",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "HEAT (register 0x02, bit 13) turns on an on-chip resistive heater, used to check the sensor, to drive condensation off it, and to reduce the offset built up after long exposure to high humidity. The heater runs only while a measurement is in progress, so a faster measurement rate heats more quickly. Peak heater current is 7.2 mA."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Power-up and I2C",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "The part is an I2C slave only, at 7-bit address 1000000; no other device on the bus may share it. SDA and SCL are open drain with spike filters and Schmitt-trigger inputs. Within 15 ms of power-up it is ready to measure; during that time only the serial-number registers (0xFB to 0xFF) can be read. Bytes go MSB first."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Reading and writing registers",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "An 8-bit pointer register picks the register. Every write starts with the address byte (R/W low) and then the pointer byte, followed by the data MSB and LSB. A read uses the last pointer written: to change it, write the address byte and pointer alone, then issue a START and the address byte with R/W high and clock in MSB then LSB. A write to a read-only register (serial, manufacturer or device ID) is answered with NACK on each data byte; a pointer to an unused address gets NACK after the pointer; a wrong device address gets NACK after the address. The pointer resets to 0x00."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Measuring temperature and humidity together",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "With MODE (bit 12) set to 1 the part converts temperature first and then humidity. Set TRES (bit 10: 0 for 14 bit, 1 for 11 bit) and HRES (bits 9:8: 00 for 14, 01 for 11, 10 for 8 bit) in register 0x02. Write the pointer 0x00 to start, wait the conversion time, then read 0x00 and 0x01 in one transaction: four bytes, temperature MSB and LSB, then humidity MSB and LSB. If the results are not ready, the address byte of the read is answered with NACK. Repeat from the trigger for further readings with the same settings."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Measuring one quantity",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "With MODE at 0 the part converts one quantity: write pointer 0x00 to start a temperature conversion or 0x01 to start a humidity conversion, wait the conversion time, then read that register. A read before the result is ready gets NACK. The result registers may be read during a conversion without disturbing it, but writing to 0x00 or 0x01 during a conversion aborts it."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Identification",
   "page": 15,
   "text": "Each part carries a unique 40-bit serial number in 0xFB to 0xFD. The manufacturer ID register (0xFE) reads 0x5449 and the device ID register (0xFF) reads 0x1050."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Placement and sample rate",
   "page": 17,
   "text": "Keep the sensor away from heat on the board, such as the battery, a display or the microcontroller. To limit self-heating, sample no faster than 1 sample/s (RH plus temperature); in home systems 0.5 or 0.2 sample/s still serves. The enclosure needs one or more openings so air reaches the sensor even when still, and reducing the board's thermal mass round the part improves response time and accuracy."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Soldering",
   "page": 18,
   "text": "Reflow to IPC/JEDEC J-STD-020 with a 260 °C peak. Use no-clean paste and do not wash the board. One IR reflow only; rework is not recommended."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Chemicals and coatings",
   "page": 18,
   "text": "Keep the sensor away from particles and from volatile chemicals such as solvents and other organic compounds. Mask the sensor while any conformal coating is applied to the board."
  },
  {
   "topic": "High temperature and humidity",
   "page": 18,
   "text": "Long exposure beyond the recommended conditions can shift the RH reading for a while: after 12, 24, 168 and 500 hours at 85 °C and 85 % RH the offset is 3, 6, 12 and 15 %. The heater helps reduce the offset."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Supply",
   "page": 19,
   "text": "Supply 2.7 V to 5.5 V, with a 0.1 µF X7R multilayer ceramic capacitor between VDD and GND, which is the only part placed next to the sensor."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Layout",
   "page": 19,
   "text": "Remove the copper planes (GND, VDD) under the part and cut a slot in the board round it, to isolate it thermally and cut thermal mass for faster response. Soldering the DAP to a board pad is allowed only if that pad is left floating, never joined to GND."
  }
 ],
